  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nAnomalous upper high over the Four Corners continues to dominate \nlocal conditions with above normal 850 mb temperatures and still \nquite humid surface conditions. Recent 24-hour surface ob change \nplots showing a warming though drying boundary layer and see no \nreason why this will not continue today with slightly warmer 850 mb \ntemp and stronger mixing progged. Highs in the upper 90s to around \n102 should be common with dewpoints in the upper 60s to mid 70s \nsupporting heat indices reaching 108 to 114 for most locations, \njustifying the going Extreme Heat Warning. Isolated convection in \neastern Nebraska recently forming on mid-level warm-air advection on \nthe nose of the low-level jet looks to remain northeast of northeast \nKansas. \n\nThe upper pattern slowly changes later today through the remainder \nof the forecast. A shortwave moving through the Great Lakes ushers a \nmodified cold front across the area late tonight into Tuesday. This \nwill drop temperatures at least a bit, but given dewpoint trends of \nlate, confidence in their specifics is not high, and could still \nbring oppressive heat. Further, a weaker wave looks to spark \nconvection over western Nebraska this afternoon, with some CAMs \nbringing weakening showers and storms into the area late tonight \ninto Tuesday, with a few members keeping some organization to \nconvection this far southeast despite increasing MLCIN. Chances for \nsevere weather via downburst winds look less than 10 percent and \nonly in portions of north-central Kansas, but lingering precip and \nconvective debris would keep conditions cooler as well. Current \nforecast values have afternoon heat indices close to Advisory levels \nin southeastern areas, bringing a fourth day of notable heat, and \nwill keep the Extreme Heat Watch going in much of east central \nKansas. \n\nUpper flow becomes more northerly by late week, with a continued \nsignal for a decent shortwave diving south-sotheast through the mid-\nMississippi River Valley Thursday into Thursday night for at least \nmoderate precipitation chances locally. Post-frontal northwest flow \nkeeps small precipitation chances in place ahead of this. \nTemperatures close to normal Wednesday and Thursday drop further \nbehind this wave for the remainder of the forecast.",
